   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the retirement and social security law, in relation to members of the twenty-year/age fifty retirement program for Triborough bridge and tunnel members",
    "basePrintNo": "S4702",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4702-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §604-c, R & SS L",
    "lawSection": "Retirement and Social Security Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4702\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the retirement and social security law, in relation to\nmembers of the twenty-year/age fifty retirement program for Triborough\nbridge and tunnel members\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSec. 1 - requires that members retiring under the TBTA 20-Year/Age 50\nPlan pay the required Additional Member Contribution (AMC) for time\ncredited toward retirement from the period service in a non-participat-\ning TBTA management title.\n\nSec. 2 - Effective Date\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nThis proposed legislation would amend Retirement and Social Security Law\nSection 604-c.e(1) to require that AMC for a period of non-participation\nbe made by a member of the Triborough Bridge and Tunnel Authority (TBTA)\n20-Year/Age 50 Retirement Plan (TBTA 20/50 Plan) who ceases to be a\nparticipant and later again becomes a participant of the TBTA 20/50\nPlan.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill is to amend an oversight in the TBTA 20/50 Plan (RSSL § 604-c)\nto address when a member leaves the plan to take a managerial (non-plan)\nposition and then subsequently return to ranks covered by the plan and\nretires under the plan. While in management positions those members stop\nmaking the AMC which can range from 5.5% - 6% depending on their rank\nprior to taking the managerial position. At a later date the member can\nreturn to the 20 year/age 50 plan rank to retire under the plan and\ninclude the time as a manager toward the 20 years required to retire\nwithout repaying the system for the non-contributory, yet credited time.\nIn many cases it has been as much as 10 years without making any AMC\ntoward their pension.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2015 Session - New Bill\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nEnactment of this proposed legislation could decrease employer costs,\nwhere such amounts would depend on the number of members affected, upon\nthe number of years that they were in non-participating positions and\ntheir gross wages during those years and would equal the value of the\nadditional AMC paid.\n\nThe increase in the assets of NYCERS as a result of the additional AMC\npaid in any year would be treated as an actuarial gain and be amortized\nover 15 years. Based on the assumptions herein, the cost savings would\nbe approximately $7,000 per year per affected member.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nImmediately.\n\n",
