    "lawCode": "Amd §604-c, R & SS L",
    "lawSection": "Retirement and Social Security Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S4702A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the retirement and social security\nlaw, in relation to members of the twenty-year/age fifty retirement\nprogram for Triborough bridge and tunnel members\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSec. 1- requires that members retiring under the TBTA 20-Year/Age 50\nPlan pay the required Additional Member Contribution (AMC) for time\ncredited toward retirement from the period service in a\nnon-participating TBTA management title.\n\nSec. 2 - Effective Date\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis proposed legislation would amend Retirement and Social Security\nLaw Section 604-c.e(1) to require that AMC for a period of\nnon-participation be made by a member of the Triborough Bridge and\nTunnel Authority (TBTA) 20-Year/Age 50 Retirement Plan (TBTA 20/50\nPlan) who ceases to be a participant and later again becomes a\nparticipant of the TBTA 20/50 Plan.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThis bill is to amend an oversight in the TBTA 20/50 Plan (RSSL §\n604-c) to address when a member leaves the plan to take a managerial\n(non-plan) position and then subsequently return to ranks covered by\nthe plan and retires under the plan. While in management positions\nthose members stop making the AMC which can range from 5.5% 6%\ndepending on their rank prior to taking the managerial position. At a\nlater date the member can return to the 20 year/age 50 plan rank to\nretire under the plan and include the time as a manager toward the 20\nyears required to retire without repaying the system for the\nnon-contributory, yet credited time. In many cases it has been as much\nas 10 years without making any AMC toward their pension.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2015 Session - New Bill\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nEnactment of this proposed legislation could decrease employer costs,\nwhere such amounts would depend on the number of members affected,\nupon the number of years that they were in non-participating positions\nand their gross wages during those years and would equal the value of\nthe additional AMC paid.\n\nThe increase in the assets of NYCERS as a result of the additional AMC\npaid in any year would be treated as an actuarial gain and be\namortized over 15 years. Based on the assumptions herein, the cost\nsavings would be approximately $7,000 per year per affected member.\nFiscal Note 2016-07 prepared March 9, 2016 by the Chief Actuary of the\nNew York City Employees Retirement System.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
